    "content" : "I have a project in mind for switching the lamps in my bedroom on and off from either the lightswitch and the lamps individually. To start off, I made an outlet that works the same as the one in the bedroom. The bottom outlet is always on and the top outlet is controlled by the lightswitch. I plan on powering a microcontroller from the always-on outlet which will also control these wireless outlets which will be connected to another outlet in the bedroom. It will detect if the top outlet is on or off. I&#39;m still in the process of figuring out the best way to detect that. I&#39;m looking for something that is power efficient and has a low part count. I think a non-contact detector will work well but I need to experiment that I can detect the top outlet&#39;s voltage, while powering from the bottom outlet.",
    "objectType" : "note",
    "originalContent" : "I have a project in mind for switching the lamps in my bedroom on and off from either the lightswitch and the lamps individually. To start off, I made an outlet that works the same as the one in the bedroom. The bottom outlet is always on and the top outlet is controlled by the lightswitch. I plan on powering a microcontroller from the always-on outlet which will also control these wireless outlets which will be connected to another outlet in the bedroom. It will detect if the top outlet is on or off. I'm still in the process of figuring out the best way to detect that. I'm looking for something that is power efficient and has a low part count. I think a non-contact detector will work well but I need to experiment that I can detect the top outlet's voltage, while powering from the bottom outlet.",
